{A similar scientific game, or rather more so, Mason being Teichmann's oponent,
wa played by the latter. He first, by gradual cautious advance of the Pawns,
hemmed in his opponent's forces, guarding every exit, and then pressed forward
with the infantry -the centre and right wing Pawns- supported by Queen, two
Rooks, and Knight. He effected a breach at last. Mason's King became exposed
and he gave in, the loss of Queen or mate being threatened. source: London
Evening Standard, 10 AUG 1904, p. 9} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5.
e3 dxc4 6. Bxc4 c6 7. Nf3 Nbd7 8. O-O Nd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Re1 Nxc3 11. bxc3 e5
12. e4 O-O 13. Qd2 h6 14. h3 exd4 15. cxd4 Rd8 16. Qb2 Nf6 17. Ne5 Be6 18. Bxe6
fxe6 19. Rac1 Rab8 20. f3 Nd7 21. Nd3 Nf8 22. Red1 Nd7 23. Qb3 Kh7 24. Qc3 Nf6
25. Nf4 Re8 26. Nd3 Qf7 27. Qe1 Qe7 28. Rc2 Nd7 29. f4 Nf6 30. g4 Nd7 31. Qg3
Nf6 32. Ne5 Nd7 33. Nd3 Nf6 34. Qf3 g6 35. Rg2 Nd7 36. g5 h5 37. f5 exf5 38.
exf5 Qe4 39. fxg6+ Qxg6 40. Nf4 Qf5 41. Qxh5+ Kg8 42. Ng6 {source: American
Chess Bulletin, 12/1904, p. 141 (notes by Hodges)} 1-0

